Extend the Windows x64->ARM64 host-shader-cc workaround to Linux cross builds

xenia-shader-cc (a build-time host tool: GLSL/XeSL -> SPIR-V -> .h codegen,
run *during* the build itself via XeniaHelpers.cmake custom commands) must
execute on the build machine regardless of what CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR the
rest of the tree is being cross-compiled for. Upstream already has this
exact problem on Windows (x64 host, ARM64 target: the cross toolchain
would otherwise produce an ARM64 PE that can't run on the x64 host) and
solves it via _xe_use_host_shader_cc: skip building xenia-shader-cc (and
its host-only glslang/SPIRV-Tools-static/-opt deps) from source, and
instead import a binary already produced by a prior *native* build.

REG-Linux hits the identical problem doing a Linux x86_64-host ->
aarch64-target Buildroot cross build (xenia-edge on e.g. sm8250):
xenia-shader-cc got compiled as an ARM64 ELF by the cross toolchain,
which then fails to execute at build time - "not found" from /bin/sh is
actually ENOENT on the missing aarch64 dynamic loader
(/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1), not a missing file; confirmed via `file` on
the built binary (ARM aarch64) and on libglslang.a's own object members
(also aarch64 - it's a genuine host/target split, not a race).

Upstream's own condition is WIN32-only - not an oversight so much as
scope: xenia-build.py's own --target-arch flag hard-refuses non-native
values on Linux ("Cross-compilation ... is only supported on Windows and
macOS"), so upstream never needed a Linux branch here. REG-Linux's
xenia-edge.mk drives this cross build a different way (CMAKE_SYSTEM_
PROCESSOR/CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME injected directly via SYSTEM_PROCESSOR env,
see 002-cmake-find-root-path.patch) rather than through --target-arch, so
that upstream guard never fires - but the same host/target split is
still needed underneath.

The host-side binary comes from a dedicated Buildroot package,
host-xenia-edge (package/emulators/xenia-edge/xenia-edge.mk), built with
the container's system gcc/g++ and a host-tools-only CMake configure
(005-host-tools-only.patch skips everything that isn't xenia-shader-cc's
own dependency chain) into its OWN independent build tree - never this
package's build/ dir. Two earlier approaches were tried and both hit real
architectural walls, not typos: (1) an in-package native pre-pass sharing
this package's own build/ dir hit CMake caching a stale cross --sysroot
across the reconfigure; (2) giving that pre-pass its own directory instead
still routed through Buildroot's target-pinned pkg-config wrapper (no env
var redirects it to anything else), which leaked an aarch64 GTK3 include
path into the "native" gcc compile and pulled in ARM SVE-only glibc math
headers. A real separate Buildroot host package sidesteps both: its own
extraction directory can't inherit cache from this package's cross
configure, and 005-host-tools-only.patch means its configure never calls
pkg-config for anything target-scoped in the first place.

xenia-edge.mk sets XE_HOST_SHADER_CC to that host package's install path
($(HOST_DIR)/usr/bin/xenia-shader-cc) as an environment variable when
running the real (aarch64) cross build - this patch's import branch reads
it via CMake's $ENV{} before falling back to upstream's own
build/host_tools/<Config>/ search (unaffected, still what a manual
`xb build --target xenia-shader-cc` on Windows or on Linux directly
without going through this Buildroot package produces).

Signed-off-by: Carlos Martins <cjom@reglinux.org>

--- a/third_party/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/third_party/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -187,11 +187,22 @@
 # the x64 host, so reuse the binary from a previous native build instead
 # (Windows-on-ARM runs x64 fine via Prism, and macOS handles its own
 # x86_64↔arm64 via Rosetta — both directions are unaffected).
+#
+# REG-Linux: same host/target split needed for Linux x86_64-host ->
+# aarch64-target Buildroot cross builds - host-xenia-edge
+# (package/emulators/xenia-edge/xenia-edge.mk) builds xenia-shader-cc
+# natively and installs it to $(HOST_DIR)/usr/bin/xenia-shader-cc,
+# independent of this package's own build tree (see the
+# XE_HOST_SHADER_CC env var check a few lines below).
 set(_xe_use_host_shader_cc FALSE)
 if(WIN32 AND CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING AND XE_TARGET_AARCH64
     AND CMAKE_HOST_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "AMD64|x86_64")
   set(_xe_use_host_shader_cc TRUE)
 endif()
+if(UNIX AND NOT APPLE AND CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING AND XE_TARGET_AARCH64
+    AND CMAKE_HOST_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "AMD64|x86_64")
+  set(_xe_use_host_shader_cc TRUE)
+endif()

 if(NOT _xe_use_host_shader_cc)

@@ -429,19 +440,40 @@

 else()  # _xe_use_host_shader_cc: x64→ARM64 cross-compile, use native build's binary

-# Native x64 build lives at <repo>/build/ (xenia-build.py: get_build_dir(None)).
-set(_xe_host_shader_cc_dir "${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/build/host_tools")
 set(_xe_host_shader_cc_any "")
-foreach(_cfg Release Checked Debug)
-  if(EXISTS "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/${_cfg}/xenia-shader-cc.exe")
-    set(_xe_host_shader_cc_any "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/${_cfg}/xenia-shader-cc.exe")
-    break()
+
+# REG-Linux: host-xenia-edge (package/emulators/xenia-edge/xenia-edge.mk)
+# builds xenia-shader-cc natively in its own independent build tree and
+# installs it to $(HOST_DIR)/usr/bin/xenia-shader-cc - stable, absolute,
+# and unrelated to this package's own build/ dir (the shared-build-dir
+# approach tried before this had its own stale-cache problems). Preferred
+# over the WIN32-native-build-dir search below when set.
+if(DEFINED ENV{XE_HOST_SHADER_CC} AND EXISTS "$ENV{XE_HOST_SHADER_CC}")
+  set(_xe_host_shader_cc_any "$ENV{XE_HOST_SHADER_CC}")
+endif()
+
+if(NOT _xe_host_shader_cc_any)
+  # Native x64 build lives at <repo>/build/ (xenia-build.py: get_build_dir(None)).
+  # WIN32's own native pre-build always names it .exe; a Linux native
+  # build run directly via xenia-build.py (not through REG-Linux's
+  # XE_HOST_SHADER_CC-setting Buildroot package above) has none.
+  set(_xe_host_shader_cc_dir "${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/build/host_tools")
+  set(_xe_host_shader_cc_ext "")
+  if(WIN32)
+    set(_xe_host_shader_cc_ext ".exe")
   endif()
-endforeach()
+  foreach(_cfg Release Checked Debug)
+    if(EXISTS "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/${_cfg}/xenia-shader-cc${_xe_host_shader_cc_ext}")
+      set(_xe_host_shader_cc_any "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/${_cfg}/xenia-shader-cc${_xe_host_shader_cc_ext}")
+      break()
+    endif()
+  endforeach()
+endif()
 if(NOT _xe_host_shader_cc_any)
   message(FATAL_ERROR
-    "Cross-compile to ARM64 needs a host xenia-shader-cc.exe but none was found "
-    "under ${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/<Config>/.\n"
+    "Cross-compile to ARM64 needs a host xenia-shader-cc but none was found "
+    "(checked $ENV{XE_HOST_SHADER_CC} and "
+    "${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/build/host_tools/<Config>/xenia-shader-cc.exe).\n"
     "  Run a native build first to produce it, e.g.:\n"
     "    xb build --target xenia-shader-cc --config=release\n"
     "  Then re-run the cross-compile.")
@@ -449,9 +481,9 @@
 add_executable(xenia-shader-cc IMPORTED GLOBAL)
 set_target_properties(xenia-shader-cc PROPERTIES
   IMPORTED_LOCATION "${_xe_host_shader_cc_any}"
-  IMPORTED_LOCATION_RELEASE "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/Release/xenia-shader-cc.exe"
-  IMPORTED_LOCATION_CHECKED "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/Checked/xenia-shader-cc.exe"
-  IMPORTED_LOCATION_DEBUG "${_xe_host_shader_cc_dir}/Debug/xenia-shader-cc.exe")
+  IMPORTED_LOCATION_RELEASE "${_xe_host_shader_cc_any}"
+  IMPORTED_LOCATION_CHECKED "${_xe_host_shader_cc_any}"
+  IMPORTED_LOCATION_DEBUG "${_xe_host_shader_cc_any}")

 endif()
